(vector
 (map
  (:adr/id "adr-2609112300-a-generator-written-to-stop-a-class-stopped-one-instance")
  (:adr/title "A generator written to stop a class stopped one instance")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-11")
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609112200-a-canned-reading-substituted-without-checking-the-measurement"))
  (:adr/body "
## What this iteration measured

Read oracle-filters, oracle-paginate and oracle-handlers selector by
selector, which is what the previous iteration named as next. The reading
found a defect big enough that the layer question became secondary.

## The finding

oracle-filters answered the SAME NUMBER for all six of its selectors in
seventeen of the twenty wave-1 pilots.

The literals it filters on -- jurisdiction and externalId -- are
com-aadhaar's field names. The component ignores a filter key its entity
does not declare and returns every row, so every selector collapsed onto
the row count. Six questions, one answer.

    com-aadhaar  5,3,5,1,0,5   its own fields, correct
    com-aave     5,3,5,1,0,5   adapted by hand, correct
    com-accela   5,0,5,5,5,5   half adapted -- jurisdiction is real here,
                               externalId is not
    com-agones   5,5,5,5,5,5   carried
    com-adyen    5,5,5,5,5,5   carried

Two independent methods agree. The static scan compares each component's
filter literals against its own entity-specs; the dynamic measurement runs
the compiled aarch64 artifact. They pick out the same repositories.

## How severe, measured rather than argued

Delete the filter entirely -- make apply-filters return its rows
unchanged -- recompile, and com-agones measures 5,5,5,5,5,5. That is
byte-for-byte what the shipped components measured.

A component with no filtering at all was indistinguishable, under the
shipped acceptance oracle, from a component with filtering. That is the
whole content of the defect and it is one command to reproduce.

## The chain

com-aadhaar was written first and its filter literals are its own fields.
com-abb-robotics was hand ported from it and carried them. com-abb-robotics
then became the generator's template -- its names are the canon, declared
in gen-whole-component.cljs. Every repository generated from that template
inherited aadhaar's field names.

The generator exists BECAUSE of this class. Its header says so: the hand
port carried com-aadhaar's expand parameter into the expand oracle, and
the fix was to derive that block from the spec table. Then the same header
asserts that every field name is derived and the class cannot be carried.

It was carried, in the block next door. oracle-filters was never in the
block list, and the token pass substitutes ENTITY names and REF-FIELD
names, not ordinary field names. A generator written to stop one instance
of a class stopped that instance.

## The fix and its floor

oracle-filters is now regenerated from the root spec, like oracle-expand.
The floor is a PAIR on ONE field: selector 1 matches every seeded row and
selector 2 matches none. A filter that matches everything and a filter
that matches nothing are the two ends, and a component that ignores the
key silently collapses them onto each other -- so the pair is exactly what
the old oracle could not express.

Selectors 3 and 4 index the non-required field that seeded-row carries the
row number in, reaching the exactly-one and no-row cases. Where every
field of the entity is required there is no such field, and the generator
omits those two selectors with a comment saying why rather than faking
them by repeating a required field.

## Reproduce

    nbb scripts/gen-whole-component.cljs 
      <com-abb-robotics>/src/abb_robotics/whole_component.kotoba 
      <repo-worktree> <src-dir>
    amu compile src/<src>/whole_component.kotoba --target aarch64-macos 
      --policy policy.edn --output wc.o
    amu extract-native wc.o --symbol oracle-filters --output f.bin
    for s in 0 1 2 3 4 5; do
      KEXE_FUEL=100000000 ./kexe-loader f.bin <offset> 1 aarch64 <caps> $s
    done

## The guard on the rollout

A component is only rewritten where the UNPATCHED generator reproduces the
shipped file byte-for-byte. That control had to be run first, and it also
caught a mistake of mine: the first attempt used com-aadhaar as the
template and produced a component whose namespace was aadhaar's. The canon
is com-abb-robotics, declared in the generator. With the right template the
diff against every shipped component is empty.

Four repositories were skipped and stay defective, each for a named reason
rather than a silent pass:

  com-aftership, com-adyen, com-airtable  the generator REFUSES them --
    every spec declares :refs {}, so no expand oracle can be derived
  com-airbus-analytx                      hand adapted, 16 added and 23
    removed lines against the generator's output; not machine rewritten

## A second defect, in one repository

com-airtable's seeded-row emits the JSON key `name` twice. seeded-row uses
the first non-required field to carry the row index and FALLS BACK to the
first required field when the entity has none; airtable's Base declares
:fields and :required identical, so the fallback repeated a key already
present.

That is the same fallback shape the generator removed from ref-field-2,
for the same consequence, documented eight lines below in the same file.
The fallback is now the `_` padding slot the under-two-required case
already used.

Note what did NOT see this. The store layer landed last iteration compares
row COUNTS, and a duplicate key inside a row does not change a count, so
that layer agreed 5 of 5 on com-airtable while the row it seeded was
malformed. A count layer is worth having and cannot see inside a row.

## The layer question, answered per selector

oracle-handlers returns i64 for fifteen of its selectors -- status codes
for 0 through 9, counts for 10 through 12 -- and str-hash for 13 and the
else arm. So it splits thirteen derivable, two refused, which is the
per-selector shape again rather than a per-layer verdict.

But two of its derivable selectors are damaged by the same carried names:
selector 6 updates with {jurisdiction: US} and measures 400, not 200,
because the field is unknown and gets rejected -- the update-success path
is never exercised. Selector 13 hashes a field that update never wrote.

So the handler layer is not ready to generate yet, and the reason is now
named rather than guessed.

## What is still refused

The three hash-valued selectors of oracle-store. The handler layer, until
its fixtures are derived the way the filters are now. The pagination layer,
which was read this iteration -- it packs a count and a boolean into one
i64 and is derivable -- but not yet generated.

The four skipped repositories above still filter on fields they do not
declare.
")))
